.TH HGET 1
.SH NAME
hget \- fetch a URL over HTTP, with request headers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hget
[
.B -v
]
[
.BI -A " addr"
]
[
.BI -h " 'name: value'"
]...
.I url
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Hget
fetches
.I url
over HTTP or HTTPS and writes the body to standard output.  It exits with
an error if the reply is not 200.
.PP
Options:
.TP
.BI -h " 'name: value'"
Send a request header.  May be repeated.
.TP
.BI -A " addr"
Dial
.I addr
instead of resolving the URL's host.  The host from the URL is still
sent as
.BR Host: ,
named in SNI and required of the certificate.
.TP
.B -v
Print the status line and the reply headers before the body.
.PP
.IR Webgrab (1)
also fetches a URL and is the better tool for fetching a page; what it
cannot do is send a header the caller chooses, which is the one thing a
cloud provider's API requires.  Google's metadata service answers
nothing without
.BR "Metadata-Flavor: Google" ,
and every authenticated call to a storage API carries an
.B Authorization
header.
.SH EXAMPLE
Read this instance's zone on Compute Engine:
.IP
.EX
hget -h 'Metadata-Flavor: Google' \e
	http://169.254.169.254/computeMetadata/v1/instance/zone
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/hget.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR httpclient (2),
.IR cloudfs (4),
.IR webgrab (1)
.SH BUGS
No redirects are followed.
